import{_ as e,o as a,c as n,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.RycU2wFZ.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agent Infrastructure Layers","description":"The layers behind an agent that can spend autonomously, the problem each layer solves, and where AgentPay fits.","frontmatter":{"title":"Agent Infrastructure Layers","description":"The layers behind an agent that can spend autonomously, the problem each layer solves, and where AgentPay fits."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"learn/03-infra-map/agent-stack.md","filePath":"learn/03-infra-map/agent-stack.md","lastUpdated":1792595354000}'),o={name:"learn/03-infra-map/agent-stack.md"};function r(i,t,l,d,h,c){return a(),n("div",null,[...t[0]||(t[0]=[s(`<h1 id="agent-infrastructure-layers" tabindex="-1">Agent Infrastructure Layers <a class="header-anchor" href="#agent-infrastructure-layers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agent Infrastructure Layers&quot;">​</a></h1><div class="warning custom-block"><p class="custom-block-title">Experimental Project</p><p>AgentPay has not undergone an independent security audit. Do not use it with real production funds.</p></div><h2 id="in-a-nutshell" tabindex="-1">In a nutshell <a class="header-anchor" href="#in-a-nutshell" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;In a nutshell&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Safe agent spending does not come from one component. It requires <strong>infrastructure layers with distinct responsibilities</strong>. AgentPay sits in the financial-control layer: it neither thinks on the agent&#39;s behalf nor transfers funds; it decides <strong>whether payment should be allowed</strong> between the two.</p><h2 id="the-whole-stack-at-a-glance" tabindex="-1">The whole stack at a glance <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-whole-stack-at-a-glance" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The whole stack at a glance&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The diagram below is a simplified teaching model. Real systems may have less distinct boundaries, but this helps locate each component.</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>┌────────────────────────────────────────────────┐</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Application &amp; task   User goal: &quot;Research NVIDIA GPUs&quot; │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Agent               Model + orchestration (what to do) │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Tool interface      Tool calls / MCP / HTTP APIs      │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Identity &amp; auth     Who is this agent; what may it do? │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ ★ Financial control Should it pay? How much? Recovery?│ ← AgentPay</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Payment protocol   How to pay (HTTP 402 / x402)       │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>├────────────────────────────────────────────────┤</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>│ Clearing &amp; settle  How funds actually move            │</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>└────────────────────────────────────────────────┘</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        Audit &amp; reconciliation span the stack</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Question answered</th><th>Typical examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Application &amp; task</strong></td><td>What goal should be completed?</td><td>Research reports, data analysis, operations automation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Agent</strong></td><td>What should happen next?</td><td>LLMs and orchestration frameworks such as LangChain and CrewAI</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tool interface</strong></td><td>How are external capabilities called?</td><td>Tool calls, MCP servers, HTTP APIs</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Identity &amp; authorization</strong></td><td>Who is calling, and what can they do?</td><td>API keys, agent profiles, role permissions</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Financial control</strong></td><td>Should it pay, how much, and what if something goes wrong?</td><td>Budgets, limits, allowlists, circuit breakers, recovery</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Payment protocol</strong></td><td>How is payment made?</td><td>HTTP 402 and x402</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Clearing &amp; settlement</strong></td><td>How do funds arrive?</td><td>On-chain stablecoin transfers, platform wallets and ledgers</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Audit &amp; reconciliation</strong></td><td>Can events be reconstructed later?</td><td>Append-only audit events, ledger reconciliation</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The first three chapters cover the layers above. This chapter shows how they fit together.</p><h2 id="which-layers-does-a-paid-call-pass-through" tabindex="-1">Which layers does a paid call pass through? <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-layers-does-a-paid-call-pass-through" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which layers does a paid call pass through?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Continuing the research-agent example, suppose it needs paid market data:</p><ol><li>The <strong>agent</strong> decides it needs market data and selects a tool.</li><li>The <strong>tool interface</strong> turns that decision into an HTTP request to the seller&#39;s API.</li><li>The seller returns <strong>HTTP 402 Payment Required</strong>, with requirements such as price and recipient. This is the <strong>payment protocol</strong> communicating.</li><li>The request reaches <strong>financial control</strong>: Is it within the per-transaction limit? Is enough budget available? Is the recipient allowlisted? Has the request rate become abnormal? <ul><li>If any check fails, reject the request; <strong>it does not proceed to later steps</strong>.</li><li>If all checks pass, reserve budget and allow it to continue.</li></ul></li><li>Only after approval does the signer create an authorization in the format required by the <strong>payment protocol</strong>.</li><li><strong>Clearing and settlement</strong> perform the transfer, either on-chain or by debiting a platform wallet.</li><li>The data is returned to the agent, while <strong>audit</strong> records why the request was allowed.</li></ol><p>The key point is that <strong>step 4 happens before step 5</strong>. The control is applied before any signature is created.</p><h2 id="why-the-layers-cannot-replace-one-another" tabindex="-1">Why the layers cannot replace one another <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-the-layers-cannot-replace-one-another" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why the layers cannot replace one another&quot;">​</a></h2><p>It may seem that one layer could simply do more. In practice, the following assumptions cause problems:</p><p><strong>&quot;Let the model control its own spending.&quot;</strong> The model is one of the components most likely to err: it can hallucinate or be influenced by prompt injection. Asking it to limit itself is asking the subject of control to supervise itself.</p><p><strong>&quot;The payment protocol verifies things, so that should be enough.&quot;</strong> A protocol checks whether a signature is valid, not whether the payment should be made. An agent manipulated into paying 20 USDC can still create an authorization that is perfectly valid at the protocol level.</p><p><strong>&quot;The identity layer knows which agent this is; restricting permissions is enough.&quot;</strong> Identity and authorization answer &quot;who are you, and may you call this interface?&quot; Questions such as &quot;how much can you still spend this month?&quot; or &quot;how many times did you call in the last minute?&quot; require stateful budgets and counters. That is the financial-control layer&#39;s job.</p><p><strong>&quot;The clearing layer only transfers funds; business rules belong elsewhere.&quot;</strong> The clearing layer usually cannot see business context. It only knows that an authorization exists and may need to be submitted on-chain. Settlement can succeed, fail, or remain unknown. Handling these outcomes requires state management above it; see <a href="./../04-agentpay-concepts/timeout-is-not-failure">Timeout Is Not Failure</a>.</p><p>Each layer solves its own class of problems. <strong>If a layer is missing, its corresponding risks have no owner.</strong></p><h2 id="what-happens-without-financial-control" tabindex="-1">What happens without financial control? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-happens-without-financial-control" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What happens without financial control?&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Failure scenario</th><th>Without financial control</th><th>With financial control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tool retries enter an infinite loop</td><td>Each retry pays until the balance is exhausted</td><td>A rate circuit breaker blocks later payments</td></tr><tr><td>Prompt injection directs payment to an unknown address</td><td>Protocol-valid payment proceeds</td><td>Recipient is not allowlisted; reject</td></tr><tr><td>Seller quotes a much higher price than expected</td><td>Sign the requested authorization</td><td>Exceeds per-transaction cap; reject</td></tr><tr><td>Concurrent requests consume the same budget</td><td>Each checks independently; combined spend exceeds limit</td><td>Atomic reservation lets only eligible requests proceed</td></tr><tr><td>Response times out after payment</td><td>Retrying may charge twice</td><td>Query the unknown result; do not pay again blindly</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These five cases correspond to concept articles later in this learning section.</p><h2 id="relationship-to-mcp-and-x402" tabindex="-1">Relationship to MCP and x402 <a class="header-anchor" href="#relationship-to-mcp-and-x402" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Relationship to MCP and x402&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The three are often mentioned together, but have different responsibilities:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Problem addressed</th><th>In a nutshell</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>MCP</strong></td><td>How an agent discovers and calls tools</td><td>A standard interface at the tool layer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>x402</strong></td><td>How payment is made over HTTP</td><td>A protocol at the payment layer</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AgentPay</strong></td><td>Whether this payment should be made and what to do if it fails</td><td>Financial control layer</td></tr></tbody></table><p>When an MCP tool is paid, the three can work in sequence: call the tool → receive 402 → AgentPay evaluates and allows the request → complete payment using x402. They complement one another rather than compete.</p><h2 id="faq" tabindex="-1">FAQ <a class="header-anchor" href="#faq" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FAQ&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Q: Do I need to deploy every layer?</strong> No. The smallest setup uses the SDK in the agent&#39;s Python code, with a local firewall and signer handling payment; both financial control and the payment protocol run locally. Add the gateway and console when you need centralized management for multiple agents, a paid API gateway for providers, or platform wallets and audit timelines.</p><p><strong>Q: Does AgentPay custody my private key or funds?</strong> For on-chain payments, the private key stays on the buyer&#39;s side; the gateway and clearing service do not handle it. A platform virtual wallet is an internal ledger account whose balance changes are recorded in the ledger. The current version does not support withdrawals.</p><p><strong>Q: Is this layering an industry standard?</strong> No. It is a simplification for learning. Projects may divide and name the layers differently; what matters is understanding that <strong>each class of problem needs an owner</strong>.</p><h2 id="next-steps" tabindex="-1">Next steps <a class="header-anchor" href="#next-steps" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Next steps&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>To learn what AgentPay does and does not do: <a href="./where-agentpay-fits">Where AgentPay Fits</a></li><li>To understand how it differs from x402: <a href="./agentpay-vs-x402">AgentPay vs. x402</a></li><li>To start with a concrete scenario: <a href="./../04-agentpay-concepts/financial-firewall">Why We Need a Financial Firewall</a></li></ul>`,33)])])}const g=e(o,[["render",r]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
